<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Welcome Handbook</title>
    <style>
        @page { size: A4; margin: 0; }
        .page {
            width: 794px; height: 1123px;
            padding: 50px 60px; box-sizing: border-box;
            overflow: hidden; page-break-after: always;
            position: relative;
            font-family: "DejaVu Serif", "Liberation Serif", Georgia, "Helvetica", Arial, sans-serif;
        }
        .page:last-child { page-break-after: auto; }
        h1, h2, h3 { font-family: "DejaVu Serif", "Liberation Serif", Georgia, "Helvetica", Arial, sans-serif; }
        .header-title { font-size: 36pt; font-weight: bold; }
        .section-title { font-size: 18pt; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 10px; }
        .subsection-title { font-size: 14pt; font-weight: bold; margin-top: 20px; margin-bottom: 5px; }
        .body-text { font-size: 11pt; line-height: 1.5; }
        .contact-info p { margin: 2px 0; font-size: 10pt; }
        .grid-container { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; margin-bottom: 30px; }
        .column { width: 48%; }
        .list-item { margin-bottom: 8px; }
        .list-item ul { padding-left: 20px; margin: 5px 0 0 0; }
        .map-container { text-align: center; margin-top: 20px; }
        .map-placeholder { border: 1px solid #ccc; width: 100%; height: 300px; background-color: #f0f0f0; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; color: #888; }
    </style>
</head>
<body>

<div class="page">
    <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: flex-start;">
        <div style="width: 50%;">
            <h1 style="font-size: 24pt; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 20px;">
                <span data-field="university_name">{{ university_name }}</span>
            </h1>
            <div class="contact-info">
                <p>Office of Admission</p>
                <p>Calle de la Villa 3</p>
                <p><span data-field="city_state">{{ city_state }}</span>, <span data-field="postal_code">{{ postal_code }}</span></p>
                <p>Tel. <span data-field="phone_number_1">{{ phone_number_1 }}</span></p>
            </div>
        </div>
        <div style="width: 45%; text-align: right;">
            <h1 class="header-title">MADRID 2021-2022<br>WELCOME HANDBOOK</h1>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div style="margin-top: 50px;">
        <div class="grid-container">
            <div class="column">
                <h2 class="section-title">
                    CONGRATULATIONS ON YOUR<br>ACCEPTANCE TO SUFFOLK MADRID!
                </h2>
                <div class="body-text">
                    <p>We look forward to welcoming you to our campus in person. In the meantime, we hope these tips prove helpful as you prepare for your time in Spain. If you have any questions that are not discussed in the following pages, please contact us.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="column">
                <div style="text-align: right; margin-top: 100px;">
                    <img src="placeholder_logo.png" alt="University Logo" style="width: 200px;">
                    <p style="font-size: 10pt; margin-top: 10px;">MADRID CAMPUS</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
    <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between;">
        <div style="width: 50%;">
            <h2 class="section-title">WHAT TO KNOW BEFORE YOU GO</h2>

            <h3 class="subsection-title">FEES AND PAYMENTS</h3>
            <div class="body-text">
                <p>For incoming freshmen and transfer students, you must confirm your decision to enroll by submitting a deposit of <span data-field="deposit_amount">{{ deposit_amount }}</span>. Please visit the Tuition & Fees page on our website for details on how to make your payment. We encourage you to pay your deposit early; the sooner we receive it, the higher your priority in class registration and housing preferences.</p>
                <p>Once you've confirmed your enrollment, you should finalize all financial arrangements before the date listed on our academic calendar.</p>
            </div>

            <h3 class="subsection-title">FINANCES</h3>
            <div class="body-text">
                <p>Like many other European Union countries, Spain uses the euro as its currency. The euro fluctuates in value against other currencies, so things will cost more or less depending on your home currency's exchange rate.</p>
                <p>Personal checks and cashier's checks cannot be cashed, and traveler's checks are no longer widely used; we recommend not bringing them.</p>
                <p>Bank cards and credit cards are very handy. Verify with your bank that you'll be able to use your card in Spain. You may withdraw money from an automatic teller machine (ATM) in euros, while your home account is debited the amount in your home currency. Make sure you check with your bank about international withdrawals so you aren't charged any unexpected fees in Spain. We also advise you to inform your bank that you'll be making transactions abroad so it won't block your account due to suspected fraud.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
        <div style="width: 50%;">
            <h2 class="section-title">SPENDING MONEY</h2>
            <div class="body-text">
                <p>Most of your expenses are covered through your tuition and fees, but you should take fixed expenses into consideration, including public transportation (prices listed below) as well as variable expenses like optional student activities, shopping, and snacks.</p>
            </div>

            <h3 class="subsection-title">Pre-Departure Financial Checklist</h3>
            <ul style="list-style-type: disc;">
                <li class="list-item">Confirm that your bank card will work in Spain and that you have a four-digit PIN. Your bank may suggest that you use a particular network. The most common networks in Spain are Servired, 48, and Euronet.</li>
                <li class="list-item">Find out how often you can make withdrawals.</li>
                <li class="list-item">Find out if there's a limit on how much money you can withdraw at one time.</li>
            </ul>

            <h3 class="subsection-title">Expenses to Keep in Mind</h3>
            <div class="body-text">
                <p>Your tuition and program fee will cover most of your expenses, but you will have to pay for the items below. Plan your budget with this in mind.</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc;">
                    <li class="list-item">Meals — Your fee includes three meals per day in both of our housing options (homestay or student residence). In most restaurants, you can have a three-course lunch ("menu del día") for about €10, a coffee costs around €1.50, and a soft drink at an outdoor cafe runs approximately €3.</li>
                    <li class="list-item">Public Transportation — Reasonably priced, unlimited monthly passes allow you to ride Madrid's excellent subway, bus, and train routes as often as you want. This monthly pass—how much per person—is based around Madrid—costs <span data-field="monthly_pass_cost">{{ monthly_pass_cost }}</span>.</li>
                    <li class="list-item">Other Costs — Tickets cost between €1.30 and €2, while ten-trip tickets cost €12. You'll get more information on how to buy transportation at our passes.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
    <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between;">
        <div style="width: 50%;">
            <h2 class="section-title">WEATHER</h2>
            <div class="body-text">
                <p>Temperatures can vary a lot in Madrid. Your time in the city will require careful packing, thoughtful planning—and maybe a shopping trip!</p>
                <p><strong>Fall Semester</strong> — Temperatures will range from quite hot when you arrive in August to cool when you leave in December. Madrid's climate is dry. Temperatures might reach as high as <span data-field="high_temp_fall">{{ high_temp_fall }}</span>°C and as low as <span data-field="low_temp_fall">{{ low_temp_fall }}</span>°C.</p>
                <p><strong>Spring Semester</strong> — It will be cold when you arrive in January and all through the winter, but May will be quite warm. Winter temperatures range from as low as <span data-field="low_temp_spring">{{ low_temp_spring }}</span>°C, while spring temperatures range from <span data-field="high_temp_spring">{{ high_temp_spring }}</span>°C.</p>
                <p><strong>Summer Session</strong> — Expect extremely hot and dry weather, with temperatures ranging from <span data-field="high_temp_summer">{{ high_temp_summer }}</span>°C to <span data-field="low_temp_summer">{{ low_temp_summer }}</span>°C.</p>
            </div>

            <h2 class="section-title">WHAT TO PACK (IF YOU CAN'T CARRY IT, LEAVE IT AT HOME!)</h2>
            <div class="body-text">
                <p>When you pack, keep in mind that Spain typically offers limited physical store space. This is especially true of Spanish closets. You should limit your luggage accordingly.</p>
                <p>Be sure to bring personal toiletries, clothes for warm and cold weather, a jacket, a winter coat, and gloves. Comfortable walking shoes and an umbrella are also travel musts. Don't bring heavy items like books, towels, or pillows, though. They'll be provided to you.</p>
                <p>If your doctor has prescribed a long-term medication, we advise you to bring a sufficient supply to last your entire stay in Spain.</p>
                <p>Spain has two power roundings; the voltage is 220. In order to use electronic items, you'll need a plug adapter, voltage converter, or both. Usually, devices like laptops, iPads, cell phones, and digital cameras automatically adapt to the 220 voltage range. If an item is marked "100/240 V," it falls within the Spanish range and will just need a plug adapter. Appliances like hair dryers and curling irons may require a voltage converter and plug adapters. You can just buy the items you need once you're in Spain.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
        <div style="width: 50%;">
            <h2 class="section-title">WHAT TO DO ONCE YOU ARRIVE</h2>

            <h3 class="subsection-title">SAFETY AT THE AIRPORT</h3>
            <div class="body-text">
                <p>Madrid is generally a very safe world capital. You'll get more detailed safety tips at orientation, but it's best to be prepared as you travel in Spain. You will most likely be jet-lagged and disoriented after departing in this new city—and therefore be more vulnerable to minor theft. Do not carry all of your valuables and important documents in one place. Try to minimize the number of bags you carry so you can control them at all times. Beware of strangers offering 'assistance.' And while it may look strange, it's safer to wear your backpack as a 'front pack.'</p>
            </div>

            <h3 class="subsection-title">ARRIVING IN MADRID</h3>
            <div class="body-text">
                <p>Please complete your Arrival Pick-Up Form online by the due date. You can also email the required information to <span data-field="arrival_email">{{ arrival_email }}</span>.</p>
                <p>All students are expected to arrive in Madrid's Barrajas Airport on the date specified on your Airport Arrival Pick-Up Form, between 7:30 a.m. and 2 p.m. We'll meet students arriving on this date and between these times, but only if we already know their airline, flight number, city of flight origin, and arrival time.</p>
                <p>Upon arriving in Madrid, you must go through passport control and then get your luggage before proceeding through customs. Watch for the "nothing to declare" signs and pass the green-unmarked police gates. Once you enter the arrival hall, you will see people waiting for passengers.</p>
                <p>Suffolk University representatives will meet students at Terminals 1 and 4. Look for a blue and gold sign with the school's name (not yours). Students arriving at Terminal 2 must proceed to Terminal 1 (Legazpi/Arnaldo) and look for our campus representatives in front of Sala 1 (Lounge 1).</p>
                <p>If your luggage is lost, remain in the baggage claim area, go to the claim counter of your airline near the baggage carousel, and fill out a claim form. The airline will give you a receipt with your claim number. You should direct your lost luggage to Suffolk (see back cover for address) or for assistance at the airport. We will help you file a claim with the airline.</p>
                <p>If you are in Madrid, we will help you with your luggage and transportation to your residence hall, as there's someone available to receive and sign for it from 9 a.m. to 9 p.m. Make sure to bring your passport and a copy of your acceptance letter to meet our staff.</p>
                <p>Your lost luggage claim suggests a significant delay or you need our help, ask the airline to call our representatives at the airport at <span data-field="airport_phone">{{ airport_phone }}</span>.</p>
                <p>After collecting your luggage, exit the baggage claim area. Look for the Suffolk University representatives and introduce yourself. We'll direct you to the shuttle that will take you to Suffolk.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
    <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between;">
        <div style="width: 50%;">
            <h2 class="section-title">DIRECTIONS TO OUR CAMPUS</h2>
            <div class="body-text">
                <p>Suffolk University Madrid Campus is located in the university district. Our building is adjacent to the intersection between the streets of Avenida del Valle and Isaac Peral, easily accessible by subway line 6 (the gray line) at the subway stop Vicente Aleixandre, or lines 6 and 7 (gray and orange lines) at the Guzmán El Bueno subway stop. Turn to the back cover for our complete address.</p>
            </div>

            <h2 class="section-title">ACADEMIC AND CULTURAL ORIENTATION</h2>
            <div class="body-text">
                <p>At the start of each semester, all new students attend orientation in Madrid. These sessions cover academic and cultural life at Suffolk Madrid, as well as safety tips and practical advice on getting around the city. Museum visits and fun activities are part of orientation too, and allow students to meet some of Spain.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="map-container">
                <div class="map-placeholder">
                    [Map Placeholder: Campus Location]
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
        <div style="width: 50%;">
            <h2 class="section-title">HEALTH INSURANCE</h2>
            <div class="body-text">
                <p>Suffolk partners with Druss Cussac, AXA, and Cultural Insurance Services International to provide immediate support for students experiencing challenges while abroad. Druss Cussac will serve as your contact for medical and security services. The international travel, accident, and sickness insurance policy includes hospital room and board, inpatient and outpatient procedures, labs and X-rays, inpatient and outpatient mental health, physician office visits, and prescription drugs. This insurance is mandatory for all students and is required for enrollment. Please read the policy document carefully, as it is not recommended.</p>
            </div>

            <h2 class="section-title">KEEPING IN TOUCH WITH YOUR FAMILY</h2>
            <div class="body-text">
                <p>In addition to using the minutes included in your cell phone package, you can email, Skype, or chat online from campus or anywhere you're connected to Wi-Fi. If you have trouble making an urgent call, our staff will happily let you use a campus phone.</p>
                <p>We recommend that letters and packages be mailed to you using Suffolk Madrid's address (on back cover), listing your name after "Suffolk University." Mail is held at the reception desk and students are alerted by email when they have received a package.</p>
                <p>Please note: We highly discourage students from receiving packages mailed from abroad. International shipping is expensive, and customs duties can be very high. If you are sending a package, we suggest that the contents and don't include medicine or electronics, b) weigh less than eight pounds (four kilos), c) add up to less than €50 in value and d) are used, not new. You may avoid import taxes too by researching online shipping options and shopping at Madrid's wide array of stores.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
    <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between;">
        <div style="width: 50%;">
            <h2 class="section-title">IF YOU'RE NOT ON GROUP ARRIVAL DAY</h2>
            <div class="body-text">
                <p>If you are arriving earlier, you'll need to make your own housing arrangements until the group arrival day. Let us know your plans so we can arrange for you to meet up with the group.</p>
            </div>

            <h2 class="section-title">TRAVEL ADVICE</h2>
            <div class="body-text">
                <p>If you're arriving later, you must let us know in advance. You must also contact the housing department at <span data-field="housing_contact">{{ housing_contact }}</span> to arrange for your housing. We strongly encourage you to make all possible efforts to arrive on the specified date so you don't miss the mandatory orientation.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
        <div style="width: 50%;">
            <div style="text-align: center; margin-top: 100px;">
                <img src="placeholder_group_photo.jpg" alt="Students Group Photo" style="width: 100%;">
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

</body>
</html>